Well, I did a little research.  I should have done so *before* posting, but 
it's too late now.  It turns out that eggs don't really cause instadeath at 
all; the Nomes just act as if they did. The Scarecrow nails the Nome King 
with a pair of eggs, and all it does is to upset him. I suppose that one 
could rationalize this by saying that, well, the Nome King is a powerful 
sorcerer, was wearing his Magic Belt and so forth, and, thus protected, was 
able to resist the destruction that any of his subjects would have suffered 
on being egged.

In another book (*Rinkitink*), Dorothy again threatens him with eggs, and he 
says that being egged causes Nomes to lose their magical invulnerability. Of 
course, NH gnomes are far from invulnerable, so this wouldn't fit well.

I was thinking about this because I had, quite simply, forgotten about gnome 
players. Instadeath on contact with eggs seems a little harsh ("You feel here 
an egg. -more- You die. DYWYPI?) Maybe eggs could have a different effect on 
PCs? (You pick up the egg. You feel vulnerable!) For consistency, eggs in the 
inventory should at least terrify monster gnomes (You wield an egg. The gnome 
king turns to flee!).

I realize that NHINO, but I do enjoy it when there is some consistency with 
well-known literary sources.

